    "field": "Conceptual climate modeling",
    "problem": "Simple climate models inadequately describe the non-constant radiative response seen in complex models because they treat feedbacks as either time-dependent (pattern effects) or state-dependent, failing to capture the combined effect that makes the system's adjustment timescales dependent on the strength of the external forcing.",
    "method": "This paper analyzes and compares four conceptual energy balance models, demonstrating that a combined model incorporating both multi-timescale dynamics (time-dependence) and a temperature-dependent feedback parameter (state-dependence) is required to adequately describe the climate response in comprehensive atmosphere-ocean general circulation models.",
    "task": "The task is to analyze and compare four simple conceptual models to explain the climate's temporal response to external forcing, distinguishing between feedbacks that are implicitly time-dependent (two-timescale) versus explicitly state-dependent on temperature.",
